   OBSERVABLE EVENT NAMES (use EXACTLY one of these when it applies — the framework has a channel that can confirm or refute it, and an event outside this list can never be scored):
     * timeout — the attempt is INTERRUPTED by the harness's wall-clock limit before it delivers a usable result; a solver that stops at its own time limit and still returns a readable result is NOT this event;
     * environment_failure — the attempt is blocked by its environment: a missing dependency or import, a licence, a permission, unavailable sandbox isolation, or an unavailable external service/backend; an ordinary code/API error is implementation_failure, not environment_failure; violating the documented script contract is also an implementation failure; a framework rejection with unconfirmed responsibility stays unknown;
-    * implementation_failure — the harness's own script/stack fails technically: code or API exceptions, abnormal solver/script exits, or a result-delivery protocol failure; a legal infeasible result is NOT a failure;
+    * implementation_failure — the selected candidate encounters an evidenced substantive code/API, model-implementation or result-delivery defect within its implementation, execution and necessary technical verification window, even if repaired; not mathematical strategy inadequacy or a confirmed environment/framework fault;
     * solver_reported_infeasible — the solver would report the model infeasible (a VERDICT about the model as given, not by itself a strategy failure);
     * task_check_failed — the answer would FAIL the declared task-result check (this is the check's outcome, NOT a verdict that the model was wrong);
     * budget_exhausted — the episode's declared budget would be exceeded. Its RANGE is the WHOLE remaining episode: from this decision's anchor, adopting the candidate and continuing to solve, check, repair and retry until the solving episode ends — whether the EPISODE budget is exceeded or a hard budget limit stops it. This is NOT about one attempt, NOT the candidate's own execution scope, and NOT a claim that the candidate alone caused the whole episode's spend. Any attempt/strategy-window candidate may predict it. Judge it only from the EPISODE-scoped condition in `execution_constraints.budget_condition` (a per-attempt latency overrun is NOT this event). When no valid episode-budget condition exists, or the remaining budget is not confirmed, you may DECLINE to predict it (omit probability) — never default it to 0, and treat a known consumption with an unmeasured declared dimension as a LOWER BOUND, not a confirmed remaining budget.
-  SCORING R CONVENTION: R is the MAXIMUM single-event probability over the three R EVENTS (implementation_failure, timeout, environment_failure) that EVERY RANKED candidate supplies with a valid probability. It is a maximum single-item RISK SCORE, NOT a joint failure probability: never combine events (no product, no sum, no union) and never report one combined failure probability in place of the three per-event numbers. Consider ALL THREE explicitly: give each a finite JSON number in [0,1] (an explicit 0 is a real forecast), or omit its probability when it has no basis and say why in basis or unsupported_fields. A missing event is UNKNOWN, never 0. A duplicate event, an out-of-range number or a non-numeric value makes THAT event unusable (unknown); it is never clamped, coerced or averaged. The harness labels each event on ONE bound attempt, so predict the candidate's own attempt, never a later retry. When no event is common to every ranked candidate the harness omits R for everyone. Other events (solver_reported_infeasible, task_check_failed, budget_exhausted) remain diagnostics and NEVER enter R. G concerns original-task acceptance given faithful execution; C counts solving/rework resources. Use the small implementation_r_evidence block: it carries the LOCAL technical cases recalled as most similar to THIS candidate (same solver interface, then similar implementation steps) plus two separate GLOBAL background blocks. Each local case gives the observed label of each of the three events on ONE bound attempt and, when a closed pair recorded it, the probability this model predicted and its Brier score. Prefer the local cases; the `global_event_base_rates` block is a wider BACKGROUND (known-label denominator only, unknown labels are not non-occurrences) and `global_prediction_performance` says how this model's past R probabilities scored. Neither global block is the candidate's conditional probability and neither replaces the local technical evidence. The three events are independent: a large maximum is a risk score, never a joint probability. Supported common base rates or similar candidate scores are allowed. A reusable tested script is evidence of technical execution only, not current business correctness. No observed failure in the history is NOT zero risk: judge the candidate's own implementation.
+  SCORING R CONVENTION: R is the MAXIMUM single-event probability common to every ranked candidate; if no event is common, omit R for all. NOT a joint failure probability: never sum or combine events. Consider ALL THREE explicitly: finite JSON [0,1], including 0, or omit with a reason. Missing, duplicate or invalid probabilities stay unknown, never coerced or averaged.
+  Implementation covers actual selection through implementation/execution/necessary technical verification completion or the next formal decision, whichever comes first. Repaired local/internal defects count. Exclude preselection work, later candidates, routine probes, expected exceptions, duplicate logs and unverified suspicions. One evidenced substantive defect supplies 1; 0 needs actual implementation and complete coverage, never final execute success alone. Timeout/environment retain bound formal scopes; other events stay diagnostics. G is acceptance given faithful execution; C counts rework resources.
+  Prefer interface/method-similar cases in implementation_r_evidence; numeric feedback requires matching versions. global_event_base_rates uses known labels only; global_prediction_performance is background, not a candidate probability. A tested script proves technical execution, not business correctness; absent historical failures do not prove zero risk.
   CANDIDATE COMPLEXITY AND TIMEOUT: assess the actual algorithm, variable domains, discrete choices and constraint coupling, not just the number of variables or solver name. For enumeration, correctly count or bound the unpruned search space from domain cardinalities before crediting pruning, preprocessing, decomposition or structural reductions actually supported by the candidate. Few variables can have a huge product of domain sizes. State which bounds are conditional; include per-node work and the implementation (including branching/search or branch-and-bound). A combinatorial upper bound alone does not establish inevitable timeout. Relate the supported reduction, algorithm/solver, environment and comparable executed history to the formal harness wall-clock limit in execution_constraints.executor.timeout_seconds; distinguish an inner solver time limit that may still deliver a usable result. Give candidate-specific probabilities only with evidence for either low or high risk; do not copy fixed default rates or a solver-reputation penalty. Near-zero risk is allowed for a demonstrably small, bounded computation; absent timeout history alone does not justify zero. If evidence is insufficient, omit probability and explain why under the existing unknown-field contract; do not invent precision or impose a risk floor. R.timeout remains interruption of the bound formal execution before a usable result. Agent analysis, probing, code writing and repeated tool calls are not timeout labels; their elapsed time belongs to C.remaining_latency_s, not a new task-timeout event.
   Do NOT invent names like "model_invalid" or "no_feasible_solution": they were retired because no channel can establish them (a solver error status or a failed check does not prove the MODEL was wrong). A business risk with no observation channel may still be described in "basis"/"notes", but it must NOT be given an invented event name that looks scoreable.
 - uncertainty: object {"execution_randomness": number in [0,1] — variance inherent to running the candidate (solver heuristics, timing), "knowledge_gap": number in [0,1] — how little evidence the prediction rests on, "basis": [text], "missing": {field: reason}, "notes": [text]}. These are your own UNCALIBRATED estimates; the framework records them as such and never as measured probabilities.
 - evidence_basis: list of strings naming the evidence keys you relied on (e.g. "retrieval_evidence.hits[0]", "capability.sources.m").
 - unsupported_fields: object {field: reason} for fields you cannot or will not predict.
